---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import ArchitectureDiagram from '../../components/architecture/ArchitectureDiagram';
import DevtoolsDiagram from '../../components/architecture/DevtoolsDiagram';

// Authored as a standalone Starlight page (not a content-collection entry) so the
// diagram can be a real Astro island: `starlight-llms-txt` renders every docs
// content entry through a container with no React renderer and would crash the
// /llms-full.txt build on an embedded framework component. A src/pages route is
// outside that collection, so the island renders normally in dev and build.
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'Architecture Overview',
    description:
      'How the client sends Chart state, the Planner proposes changes, and the Engine validates a response for the client to apply.',
    // A src/pages route is not a content-collection entry, so Starlight cannot
    // auto-derive these the way it does for sibling docs. Set them explicitly:
    // the GitHub edit link, and prev/next pagination to the neighbouring
    // Understanding pages (matching the sidebar order).
    editUrl:
      'https://github.com/maelstrom-co/maelstrom/edit/main/apps/docs/src/pages/understanding/architecture.astro',
    prev: { link: '/understanding/comparison/', label: 'How Maelstrom compares' },
    next: {
      link: '/understanding/voice-architecture/',
      label: 'The voice boundary',
    },
  }}
>
  <p>
    Maelstrom turns a user's intent into a UI update. The client sends the current
    Chart state and update reasons to an Engine host. A configured Planner
    proposes changes, the Engine validates them, and the client applies the
    accepted response. Play the diagram to follow a request, or select a package
    to inspect its role.
  </p>

  <ArchitectureDiagram client:load />

  <h2>The round-trip, in words</h2>

  <h3>It starts with a reason</h3>
  <p>
    Every update begins with an <code>UpdateReason</code>, and there are three: a
    <code>user_intent</code> (someone asks for something), a
    <code>chart_resize</code> (the available grid changed), or a
    <code>vessel_mutation</code> (an Instance changed its active Variant).
    <a href="/understanding/llm-orchestration/"><code>@maelstrom-co/client</code></a>
    batches the pending reasons and builds a <code>ChartRequest</code>. This
    snapshot contains Instance identities and Variants, the current layout,
    Chart dimensions in grid cells, registered Vessels, and the layout mode. A
    <code>ChartRequestMessage</code> carries it with a session ID, message ID,
    and timestamp.
  </p>

  <h3>The Planner proposes and the Engine validates</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/understanding/engine/"><code>@maelstrom-co/engine</code></a> holds
    no client layout state. It loads prior turns when history is configured and
    passes the request to a Planner. The reference LLM Planner builds prompts
    and calls a provider adapter; another Planner can use a different decision
    process. The Engine validates the candidate and repairs recoverable layout
    and Action problems.
  </p>
  <p>
    The accepted <code>MaelstromResponse</code> contains a <code>userMessage</code>
    for the user, a <code>rationale</code> for diagnostics, an
    <code>executionPlan</code>, and a <code>layout</code>. Its
    <code>requestId</code> identifies the originating message.
  </p>

  <h3>The client applies it</h3>
  <p>
    The client dispatches <code>set_layout</code> and <code>execute_actions</code>
    as one atomic command batch, with layout first. A tiling response can introduce
    an Instance and request an Action on it in the same response. Applying the
    layout first establishes that Instance before the client waits for its
    Action runner. The runner must register before the readiness timeout;
    otherwise, the client skips the Action.
  </p>

  <h3>The loop closes</h3>
  <p>
    Applying the response updates the UI. The Chart's <code>ResizeObserver</code>
    watches its container. When the container's pixel size changes enough to
    change its whole-cell dimensions, the client sends a new
    <code>chart_resize</code> request. That starts a separate Engine turn.
    The client performs no inline size-reconciliation pass after Actions;
    the Chart clips visual overflow with CSS.
  </p>

  <h2>The vocabulary</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><strong>Vessel</strong></dt>
    <dd>
      A registered UI definition with an ID, a description, and a set of Variants.
      The Planner uses these definitions to choose layouts and Actions. See the
      <a href="/understanding/vessel-state-machine/">vessel state machine</a>.
    </dd>
    <dt><strong>Variant</strong></dt>
    <dd>
      One discrete state of a Vessel, carrying its props, the Actions available in
      that Variant, and a <code>minSize</code> enforced in grid mode.
    </dd>
    <dt><strong>Action</strong></dt>
    <dd>
      An operation the client runs on an Instance. It may move the Instance to
      another Variant via <code>transitionsTo</code>.
    </dd>
    <dt><strong>Chart</strong></dt>
    <dd>
      The container that displays Instances. Grid layouts use integer cells
      (<code>rows</code> × <code>columns</code>). Tiling, the default, uses a tree
      of <code>H</code>/<code>V</code> splits. The client converts either layout
      to pixel bounds using the container dimensions.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p>
    <code>protocol</code> defines the shared contracts. <code>client</code> manages
    requests and applies responses; <code>react</code> binds it to React.
    <code>engine</code> coordinates planning and validation on the server, and
    <code>planner-llm</code> provides the reference LLM Planner.
  </p>

  <h2>Observe the workflow with DevTools</h2>
  <p>
    DevTools observes the existing workflow. Its core package owns the bounded
    collector, explicit content capture, and shared browser panel. The React
    adapter owns attachment and mounting. Engine evidence crosses through the
    protocol only when the server host authorizes forwarding for that connection.
    Select an evidence path below to see who owns each step.
  </p>

  <DevtoolsDiagram client:load />

  <p>
    The default production entrypoints are inert. Explicit
    <code>/production</code> imports enable browser tools in every build;
    production Engine forwarding stays disabled. Follow the
    <a href="/guides/devtools/">DevTools guide</a> for installation and recording.
  </p>

